text:
I have a proposal change for @cart/hmsc-int/PaintCanvas.tsx; I’d make it one Painter with multiple editable channels, not one giant merged system.

The important distinction: keep the underlying systems separate — tiles, heightfield, placements, zones, roads — because their data, stamping, preview sync, undo semantics, and performance needs are genuinely different. But make the interaction model feel like one continuous painter. Right now the file already has the right raw ingredients: Layer drives which rail appears, which overlay captures input, which cursor is shown, and which stamp function runs; there is also already a “unified brush dispatch” seam in the middle of PaintCanvas. So I’d lean into that seam rather than trying to smash all the data structures together.

The model I’d propose is:

One active tool. One active target. Many visible channels.

So instead of thinking:

paint mode / height mode / place mode / zone mode / road mode

think:

Painter tool = Select | Paint | Erase | Place | Path

and

Painter target = Tile | Terrain | Zone | Object | Road

The bottom “layer switch” becomes a target dock or channel dock. The user is always in the same Painter, but they choose what the brush is currently editing. Tiles, height, zones, roads, and objects can all remain visible as contextual overlays. The active target gets high contrast; inactive channels become dim landmarks. This makes the world feel continuous instead of disappearing/reappearing every time the author changes modes.

For example:

When the target is Tile, the brush paints tile indices.

When the target is Terrain, the same brush edits height, ramps, slopes, or smoothing.

When the target is Zone, the brush paints zone membership.

When the target is Object, the brush cursor becomes a footprint rectangle and stamps the armed object.

When the target is Road, the brush/path tool lays road points and the road wire overlay becomes prominent.

The UI still has conditionals, but they become “what does this target need?” rather than “which mode am I in?”

I would structure the refactor around a PainterCapability registry.

Something like:

type PainterTarget = 'tile' | 'terrain' | 'zone' | 'object' | 'road';

type PainterTool = 'select' | 'paint' | 'erase' | 'place' | 'path';

interface PainterCapability {
  target: PainterTarget;

  canUseTool: (tool: PainterTool, ctx: PainterContext) => boolean;

  cursor?: (pt: ScreenPoint, ctx: PainterContext) => BrushVis;

  begin?: (pt: GraphPoint, ctx: PainterContext) => void;
  sample?: (pt: GraphPoint, ctx: PainterContext, touched: Set<ChunkKey>) => void;
  end?: (ctx: PainterContext) => boolean;

  click?: (pt: GraphPoint, ctx: PainterContext) => void;
  select?: (pt: GraphPoint, ctx: PainterContext) => PainterSelection | null;

  note?: (ctx: PainterContext) => EditNote;
}

Then you replace the repeated conditionals like:

if (layer === 'height') ...
else if (layer === 'zone') ...
else if (layer === 'paint') ...

with:

const activePainter = painterCapabilities[state.target];

activePainter.begin?.(point, ctx);
activePainter.sample?.(point, ctx, touched);
activePainter.end?.(ctx);

That gives you one input pipeline while preserving each target’s special behavior.

The biggest concrete win would be collapsing these separate overlays:

{showBrush ? <Pressable ... /> : null}
{showSelect ? <Pressable ... /> : null}
{layer === 'road' ? <Pressable ... /> : null}

into a single PainterInputOverlay.

Right now the canvas has separate screen-space input surfaces for brush, select, and road. That is exactly what creates the “separate mode” feeling. I’d make one overlay that always exists over the working area and routes events through a resolver:

const behavior = resolvePainterBehavior({
  tool,
  target,
  brush,
  place,
  selected,
  modifiers,
});

<PainterInputOverlay
  behavior={behavior}
  onPoint={screenToGraph}
  onClaimFocus={claimWasd}
/>

The resolver decides:

if (tool === 'select') return selectBehavior;
if (target === 'road') return roadPathBehavior;
if (target === 'object' && tool === 'paint') return objectStampBehavior;
if (target === 'terrain') return terrainBrushBehavior;
if (target === 'zone') return zoneBrushBehavior;
return tileBrushBehavior;

That preserves all the current conditional richness, but the user is no longer conceptually entering separate layers. They are just changing the brush’s target.

I’d also change the rail from “switch the whole rail by layer” to “compose cards.”

Current shape:

{layer === 'paint' || layer === 'height' || layer === 'zone' ? <BrushRail /> : null}
{layer === 'place' ? <PlaceRail /> : null}
{layer === 'road' ? <RoadRail /> : null}

Proposed shape:

<PainterRail>
  <ToolCard />
  <TargetCard />
  <SelectionCard />

  {usesBrush(target) ? <BrushCard /> : null}
  {target === 'tile' ? <TilePaletteCard /> : null}
  {target === 'terrain' ? <TerrainCard /> : null}
  {target === 'zone' ? <ZoneCard /> : null}
  {target === 'object' ? <ObjectBrushCard /> : null}
  {selectedObject ? <ObjectInspectorCard /> : null}
  {target === 'road' || selectedRoad ? <RoadCard /> : null}
</PainterRail>

This is probably the single most important UX change. The rail should not disappear and become a totally different instrument. It should feel like the same instrument exposing different controls based on the current target and selection.

I’d make the visual hierarchy work like this:

Tiles are always the base.

Height is always present through shading/contour/intensity, but gets stronger when Terrain is the active target.

Placements are always visible as ghost outlines unless explicitly hidden. When Object is active, they become interactive and saturated.

Zones are tint overlays that can be shown/hidden. When Zone is active, zone tint becomes stronger and the active zone cursor uses the zone color.

Roads are always stamped into tiles, but road wires/dots only become prominent when Road is active or a road is selected.

Selection highlights are universal. A pointer click should select the most specific thing under the cursor: object/build piece first, road second, cell third. That way the Select tool feels global.

So the user experience becomes:

“I am using the Painter. I can see the whole authored world. I choose what my stroke affects.”

Not:

“I am switching into a different editor.”

I would not fully remove conditionals. I’d move them into three places:

First, a capability registry for behavior.

Second, a render emphasis plan for visibility/opacity.

Third, rail cards for target-specific controls.

That keeps the PaintCanvas component from turning into an even larger pile of layer === ... checks.

A practical migration path:

Start by renaming conceptually, not structurally. Keep Layer for compatibility, but treat it as activeTarget in the UI. Change button labels and copy first: “Tile / Road / Terrain / Object / Zone” instead of “PAINT / ROAD / HEIGHT / PLACE / ZONE.”

Then extract the input overlay. Make one PainterInputOverlay that handles mouse down/move/up/leave and internally calls the current brush/select/road behavior. This is the biggest architectural cut.

Next, extract the stamping functions into target capabilities:

const tileCapability = {
  sample: stampTileAtGraph,
  note: () => ...
};

const terrainCapability = {
  begin: beginRampOrSlopeMaybe,
  sample: stampHeightOrSmoothMaybe,
  end: finishRampOrSlopeMaybe,
  note: () => ...
};

const zoneCapability = {
  sample: stampZoneAtGraph,
  note: () => ...
};

const objectCapability = {
  sample: stampObjectAtGraph,
  cursor: objectFootprintCursor,
  note: () => ...
};

const roadCapability = {
  click: roadClickAt,
  note: () => ...
};

Then replace stampOneAtGraph, strokeNote, showBrush, showSelect, and layer === 'road' overlay logic with calls into the active capability.

After that, refactor the rail. Do not rewrite BrushRail, PlaceRail, and RoadRail all at once. Split them into smaller cards and mount the cards conditionally inside one PainterRail.

Finally, replace the bottom layer switch with a channel dock:

<TargetChip label="Tile" active={target === 'tile'} visible={visible.tile} />
<TargetChip label="Road" active={target === 'road'} visible={visible.road} />
<TargetChip label="Terrain" active={target === 'terrain'} visible={visible.terrain} />
<TargetChip label="Object" active={target === 'object'} visible={visible.object} />
<TargetChip label="Zone" active={target === 'zone'} visible={visible.zone} />

Each chip can have two concepts: click to make active, small eye/lock affordance to show/hide or protect that channel. That will make it feel like one painter with channels, not five editors.

The main caution: do not make everything fully interactive all the time. That will feel cohesive for five minutes and then become accidental-click chaos. I’d use these rules:

Select tool can select anything visible.

Paint tool only edits the active target.

Eraser only erases the active target.

Objects only drag when Object is active or already selected.

Road wires are visible when Road is active, selected, or pinned.

Inactive overlays are visible but low-opacity.

This gives cohesion without ambiguity.

The clean mental model is:

Painter = input system.
Target = what the stroke edits.
Channel visibility = what the author can see.
Selection = what the inspector edits. 
lets build a plan around this idea
